{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":6313442,"reputation":46169,"user_id":5625547,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/oqCND.jpg?s=256","display_name":"0stone0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5625547/0stone0"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1713367717,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115181,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<code>json=$(cat service-data.json)</code>; 1) no need for this, just pass it as arg to jq: <code>jq &#39;some-filter&#39; service-data.json</code>"},{"owner":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1713367756,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115189,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<code>echo $json</code> is itself buggy."},{"owner":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1713367829,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115204,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"BTW, <code>&quot;export \\(.key)=\\(.value)&quot;</code> has serious security problems. <b>Always</b> use the <code>@sh</code> filter in jq to shell-quote strings before dumping them into a context where they&#39;ll be evaluated as code."},{"owner":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1713367914,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115225,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"...you don&#39;t want <code>&quot;description&quot;: &quot;Prevent users from injecting $(rm -rf ~) or &#39;$(rm -rf ~)&#39; into commands&quot;</code> to actually run <code>rm -rf ~</code>"},{"owner":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1713368168,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115279,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Beyond that, <code>declare export</code> doesn&#39;t make sense. If you want everything to be exported, make it <code>declare -x</code> -- take out the <code>export</code>."},{"owner":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1713368254,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115297,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"...looking back again, that failure to use <code>@sh</code> is the <i>immediate</i> cause of your problem -- it&#39;s not making it clear that the spaces that are data need to be literal data rather than syntax."},{"owner":{"account_id":980746,"reputation":8778,"user_id":1000510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":76,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Bhpgn.png?s=256","display_name":"Basit","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1000510/basit"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1713368501,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115340,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@CharlesDuffy Thanks for the reply. As I said I am new to script and jq both. It&#39;s just a start. After getting the values I will create a <code>systemd</code> service with these values. Here I am just making sure that I am getting the correct values so in the service all work well. I am not getting the <code>@sh</code> thing. can you provide some link so I can take a look at it. Thanks"},{"owner":{"account_id":8002,"reputation":298852,"user_id":14122,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5e2861b08f37fa306fbf5384994af688?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Charles Duffy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/14122/charles-duffy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1713369329,"post_id":78342087,"comment_id":138115498,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"See <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/69502302/14122\">stackoverflow.com/a/69502302/14122</a> for an example of using <code>@sh</code>. BTW, you might consider trace logging (<code>bash -x yourscript</code> or putting <code>set -x</code> within that script&#39;s text) to be useful here to get a view of what&#39;s going on at runtime."}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":223}